NAME
    make - build targets from a Makefile

SYNOPSIS
    make [-n] [-B] [-f file] [target ...]

DESCRIPTION
    Reads Makefile in the current directory (or file) and builds the
    targets named, or the first rule's target. A Makefile has three
    kinds of line; blank lines and lines starting with # are skipped:

        NAME = text          a variable
        target: prereq ...   a rule: what must be made (or exist) first
        <TAB>command         its recipe: the lines right below it

    In prerequisites and recipe lines $(NAME) is a variable's text, $@
    the target, $< the first prerequisite and $$ a $.

    Y1/OS keeps no file dates, so a target is built when the file does
    not exist, when one of its prerequisites was built in this run, or
    always with -B. A prerequisite with no rule must exist. A target
    with no recipe (all:) is made when any of its prerequisites is.
    To rebuild one program after editing its source: make -B name, or
    del name and make name.

    make runs nothing itself. The recipe lines of every target to
    build go, in order, into make.sh in the current directory, and make
    EXECs it: the shell runs it as a script (man sh), each line shown
    as it runs, and the first command that fails stops it.

    -n prints the recipe lines instead (nothing is written or run).
    -B builds every target reached. -f reads another file.

    A recipe may cd and run make again: that make's make.sh runs, then
    the first goes on (/src/Makefile does this for every directory).

THE SOURCES ON THE DISK
    /src holds the sources of /bin, /lib/cc and the OS, at their paths
    in the repository, each directory with a Makefile:

        cd /src/os/commands        make name, make all, make install
        cd /src/os/commands-asm    asm, /bin/asm from its assembly
        cd /src/software/compiler/c/target
                                   cc1 .. cc9, the compiler's passes
        cd /src                    make: all three; make install

    install copies the programs to /bin or /lib/cc. A command takes
    minutes at 1 MHz; the passes much longer.

LIMITS
    12,000 bytes of Makefile, 150 rules, 40 variables; one target a
    rule; a recipe line expands to 128 characters at most. No pattern
    rules, no include. make.sh needs the write handle: make inside > or
    a pipe cannot write it.

EXAMPLES
    make -n             what would run
    make touch          build one command here
    make -B touch       again, after editing touch.c

NOTES
    Added 2026-11-03 (os/commands/make.c), after P8X's make, which is a
    shell built-in that always rebuilds.

SEE ALSO
